Your task is to find a minimum-price flight route from Syrjälä to Metsälä. You have one discount coupon, using which you can halve the price of any single flight during the route. However, you can only use the coupon once.
When you use the discount coupon for a flight whose price is x, its price becomes x/2(floor) (it is rounded down to an integer).

Input:
The first input line has two integers n and m: the number of cities and flight connections. The cities are numbered 1,2,...,n. City 1 is Syrjälä, and city n is Metsälä.
After this there are m lines describing the flights. Each line has three integers a, b, and c: a flight begins at city a, ends at city b, and its price is c. Each flight is unidirectional.
You can assume that it is always possible to get from Syrjälä to Metsälä.

Output:
Print one integer: the price of the cheapest route from Syrjälä to Metsälä.

Constraints:
2<=n<=10^5
1<=m<=2 \cdot 10^5
1<=a,b<=n
1<=c<=10^9

Strategy:
No new algorithms this time. Just dijkstra state tracking. The problem requires us to find the cheapest path from 1 to n while being able to apply one discount coupon. The key is realizing that we can simulate it by adding one more dimension to the distance and other state tracking structures and just work with one type of paths which have the coupon already applied and therefore cannot have it applied again and the second one which branches every edge to the reality where we have applied the coupon right there and go to the applied reality and the other one where we saved it for next time. This way we explore all possible viable combinations. The state tracking is kinda simple. Instead of tracking just the node and its distance we also track its coupon applied/not-applied state. This means just adding one more field to the data structure. Then in the while loop for dijkstra we just implement the branching. For the nodes popped from the queue which are already in the layer where the coupon was applied it is easy. We can treat this like a normal node and just take the distance to that id in layer applied and do the classic calculation. For layer 0 (unapplied coupon) we need to do 2 things. We need to create the branch where we apply the coupon which means pushing {dist[nodeId]+edge.cost/2, nodeId, layer 1} to the queue and then we run the normal dijkstra for this one also where we just push it with the normal calculation. For both of these the classic greedy rule applied that we do it only if it would make the path to the node at the end of the edge actually shorter. At the end we output the distance to node n in layer 1 (applied).
Notes: Just like classic dijkstra, we check if the node in the queue is not already outdated and if it is we skip it. We use a 2D array of size (2,n+1) for the nodes and ll for distance so we don't overflow.